  "plan": {
    "goal": "Recapitulate the low physiological shear of lymphatic endothelium in a perfused microfluidic channel.",
    "rationale": "Lymphatic endothelium experiences markedly lower shear than blood microvasculature — roughly 0.05–0.5 dyn/cm² (0.005–0.05 Pa), with collecting lymphatics at the low end. The physiology registry resolves lymphatic endothelial cells to the HUVEC/microvascular entry (shear 0.1–1 Pa, seed 1e4–2e4 cells/cm²), but that is the blood-vascular convention; for a lymphatic model we deliberately target the low end of the lymphatic range at 0.01 Pa (0.1 dyn/cm²). In a 400 µm × 100 µm × 20 mm channel with water-like medium (1e-3 Pa·s), flow_rate_for_shear_stress gives 0.4 µL/min for 0.01 Pa. This yields Re ≈ 0.03 (deeply laminar), a 120 s residence time, and a ~4 Pa pressure drop — all easily supplied by a syringe pump. Cells are seeded at 15,000 cells/cm² (mid-registry range) on the 0.08 cm² channel floor (~1,200 cells).",
